var e=1,t=`der-vol-01`,n=`derivatives`,r=`Implied Versus Realised Volatility`,i=`The market's forecast of movement is persistently higher than the movement that arrives — and that gap is a premium paid for insurance.`,a=`advanced`,o=[`der-greeks-01`],s=9,c=[`CAIA-L2`,`CFA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`Two volatilities get discussed and they answer different questions.

**[[realised-volatility|Realised volatility]]** is measured from what actually happened: the [[standard-deviation|standard deviation]] of returns over a past window. Backward-looking, computable, unambiguous.

**[[implied-volatility|Implied volatility]]** is extracted from option prices: the [[volatility]] that makes a pricing model produce the observed premium. Forward-looking, and it is a price rather than a measurement.

**Implied volatility is what the market charges for movement. Realised is what it got.**

The consistent empirical finding is that **implied exceeds realised on average**, across markets and decades. The gap is the **[[variance-risk-premium|variance risk premium]]**, and it is the reason systematic option selling has a positive expected return.

This is not a market inefficiency. It is the price of insurance. Buyers of options are paying above the expected cost of the protection because the protection is worth more to them than its actuarial value — which is exactly why anyone buys insurance at all, and exactly why insurers make money on average.`},{type:`concept`,id:`the-surface`,body:`**Implied volatility is not one number. It differs by strike and by expiry, and the pattern is informative.**

**The skew.** Equity index puts trade at higher implied volatilities than calls at equivalent distances. Demand for downside protection exceeds demand for upside participation, and crashes are faster than rallies. The skew is a price for that asymmetry, and it is persistent rather than an anomaly.

**The term structure.** Implied volatility differs by expiry. Usually upward sloping in calm markets — more uncertainty further out — and it inverts in a crisis, when near-term fear exceeds long-term.

**Two practical readings follow.**

**An inverted volatility term structure is a stress signal**, and one of the cleaner ones available: it says the market expects the immediate future to be more violent than the distant one, which is the definition of an acute event.

**Skew steepening indicates rising demand for protection**, often before spot prices move. It is not a timing signal, and it does show where the anxiety is.

**And for anyone selling options: the skew means the options that look most expensive are the ones that pay out in exactly the scenario that hurts your other holdings.** The premium is high because the risk is genuinely correlated with everything else you own.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`insurance-not-anomaly`,body:`**Selling volatility is an insurance business, and every insight follows from taking that seriously.**

An insurer collects premiums that exceed expected claims. They make money most years. Occasionally a hurricane arrives and they pay out a decade of premiums at once. Nobody calls that a failed model — it is the model, and the discipline lies in reserving and sizing rather than in avoiding claims.

Option selling has an identical structure with one important difference: **an insurance company knows it is an insurer.** A fund selling options for "yield" frequently does not, and the difference shows up in three specific places.

**Reserving.** An insurer holds capital against the claim. A yield-focused seller often holds capital against the *expected* loss, which is a different and much smaller number.

**Correlation.** Insurers deliberately diversify across uncorrelated perils. Option sellers on equity indices are writing one peril many times — and it is the same peril that damages the rest of the portfolio.

**Reporting.** An insurer reports claims reserves. A fund reports a [[sharpe-ratio|Sharpe ratio]], which describes the premium collection and not the liability accepted.

**So the question to any short volatility strategy is the question you would ask an insurer: what is the maximum claim, what capital is held against it, and how correlated is it with everything else on the book?** Those three questions are answerable, and the answers are the strategy.`},{type:`example`,id:`worked-premium`,body:`Implied volatility on a one-year index option is 20%. Realised volatility over the year turns out to be 16%.`,walkthrough:[`The seller charged for 20% of movement and 16% arrived. The gap of **4 volatility points** is the premium earned.`,`In variance terms — which is how the payoff actually works — implied variance is 0.20² = **0.0400** against realised 0.16² = **0.0256**.`,`The ratio is **1.56**: the seller was paid for 56% more variance than occurred.`,`**Read that carefully: a 4-point volatility gap is a 56% variance gap, because the payoff is in squared terms.** Volatility differences understate the economics considerably.`,`Now the other side. If realised volatility had come in at 30% rather than 16%, implied variance of 0.0400 against realised 0.0900 means the seller paid out **more than twice** what they collected.`,`And that asymmetry is structural: realised volatility has a floor at zero and no ceiling, so the seller's gain is bounded and their loss is not.`,`Sanity check worth reusing: **compare implied volatility with the realised volatility of the same asset over a comparable past window.** If implied is far above, you are being paid well to sell — and it is usually high because something is genuinely uncertain.`]},{type:`table`,id:`vol-table`,caption:`Reading the volatility surface`,headers:[`Observation`,`What it usually means`,`What it is not`],rows:[[`Implied well above realised`,`A high variance risk premium — good compensation for sellers`,`A free lunch; it is usually high for a reason.`],[`Steep put skew`,`Strong demand for downside protection`,`A directional forecast.`],[`Inverted term structure`,`Acute near-term stress expected`,`A signal about the level of prices.`],[`Implied below realised`,`The market is underpricing movement — good for buyers`,`Common; it happens mainly after volatility has already risen.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-goes-wrong`,body:`**Selling volatility because the premium is high.** Implied volatility rises when uncertainty rises. The compensation is larger because the risk is larger, and the two usually move together.

**Sizing on average outcomes.** The expected loss is not the loss that matters. Position sizing for a short volatility book has to be driven by the tail, or the strategy is a series of good years followed by one that ends it.

**Ignoring correlation with the rest of the portfolio.** Equity index option selling loses precisely when equities fall, which is when everything else in a typical portfolio is also losing. It is negatively diversifying at the worst moment.

**Confusing the VIX with a tradeable asset.** Volatility indices are not investable directly; the instruments that track them hold futures and carry roll costs that can dominate returns over any long holding period.

**Treating realised volatility as the benchmark for a hedge's cost.** A protective put costs the premium, and comparing that with realised volatility after the fact is hindsight — the point of the hedge was the outcome you did not get.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`vol-in-a-portfolio`,body:`**Short volatility exposure appears in an alternatives portfolio in more places than anyone has usually counted.**

The explicit version is a fund that sells options. The implicit versions are more common and rarely aggregated:

**[[merger-arbitrage|Merger arbitrage]]** — collect a small spread; lose a lot if the deal breaks. Short a deal-break option.

**[[fixed-income-relative-value|Fixed income relative value]]** — small convergence gains, large loss if the relationship dislocates.

**Selling credit protection or holding levered credit** — collect the spread, take the default.

**Illiquid strategies with stable marks** — earning a premium for accepting liquidity risk that materialises all at once.

**Covered call overlays** — selling upside for premium.

**Every one of these has the same shape: frequent small gains, infrequent large losses.** Individually each is defensible. **Aggregated without being recognised, they are one position expressed many ways** — and its bad scenario is a single event.

The practical test is the one that cuts through the labels: **what does each strategy do in a 20% equity drawdown with widening spreads?** Strategies that all answer "badly" are the same strategy, whatever their names, and a correlation matrix built on calm periods will not show it.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Compare implied volatility with realised over a comparable window before selling any option.** High implied usually reflects genuine uncertainty rather than a mispricing.

**Ask a short volatility manager the three insurance questions:** maximum claim, capital held against it, and correlation with the rest of the book.

**Count the implicit short volatility positions across the whole portfolio.** Merger arb, levered credit, relative value and illiquid strategies with smooth marks all belong on the list.

**Run the single stress: 20% equity drawdown, spreads doubling, liquidity gone.** Every strategy that loses is part of the same exposure.

**Treat an inverted volatility term structure as information about stress, not about direction.** It says the market expects violence soon, which is a different claim from expecting prices to fall.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Implied volatility is a price for expected movement; realised is what arrived. Implied exceeds realised on average, and that variance risk premium is payment for insurance rather than an inefficiency.`,`A 20% implied against 16% realised is a 4-point volatility gap and a 56% variance gap — the payoff is in squared terms, so volatility comparisons understate the economics.`,`Selling volatility is an insurance business: size for the maximum claim, hold capital against it, and check how correlated the peril is with the rest of the book.`,`Short volatility hides throughout an alternatives portfolio — merger arb, levered credit, relative value, illiquidity premia. Ask what each does in one 20% drawdown scenario.`]}],u=[{id:`der-vol-01-q1`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:2,tags:[`volatility`,`definition`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`What is implied volatility?`,choices:[`The standard deviation of an asset's returns over the last year`,`The volatility that makes a pricing model reproduce an option's observed market price`,`The maximum volatility an asset has experienced`,`A regulatory measure of portfolio risk`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`That is realised volatility, measured from history rather than extracted from a price.`,`Correct. It is backed out of the market price, which makes it a price for expected movement rather than a measurement of past movement.`,`No such definition exists; historical maxima are a separate statistic.`,`It is a market-derived quantity, not a regulatory construct.`],explanation:`The distinction matters because implied volatility is a price and behaves like one — it contains a risk premium and moves with supply and demand for protection, not only with expectations about movement.`},{id:`der-vol-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`volatility`,`variance`,`arithmetic`],concept:`worked-premium`,stem:`Implied volatility is 20% and realised turns out to be 16%. What is the ratio of implied variance to realised variance? Two decimal places.`,answer:1.56,tolerance:.04,toleranceType:`abs`,inputHint:`Square each volatility, then divide.`,explanation:`A 4-point volatility gap becomes a 56% variance gap because the payoff is in squared terms. Anyone assessing option-selling economics in volatility points rather than variance is understating both the gain and the loss.`},{id:`der-vol-01-q3`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`volatility`,`premium`],concept:`insurance-not-anomaly`,stem:`Why does implied volatility persistently exceed realised volatility?`,choices:[`Because option pricing models are systematically wrong`,`Because option buyers pay above the actuarial cost for protection, exactly as insurance buyers do`,`Because realised volatility is measured incorrectly`,`Because market makers collude`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`The models are calibrated to prices; the gap is in the prices themselves and reflects a preference.`,`Correct. Protection is worth more than its expected cost to someone who needs it, so the premium exceeds the actuarial value. That is the definition of an insurance market working normally.`,`Realised volatility is straightforward to measure and the gap survives any reasonable method.`,`The premium appears across markets, venues and decades, which no collusion explanation covers.`],explanation:`Framing it as insurance rather than as an anomaly changes the conclusion: the premium should persist, because the demand for protection is structural. It also means the seller's job is reserving and sizing, not predicting.`},{id:`der-vol-01-q4`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`volatility`,`misconception`],concept:`how-it-goes-wrong`,stem:`"Implied volatility is unusually high, so selling options now is especially attractive." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — implied volatility rises when uncertainty rises, so the higher compensation usually reflects genuinely higher risk rather than a better deal`,`False — implied volatility being high means options are cheap`,`True — a higher premium always improves the expected return of selling`,`False — because implied volatility cannot be observed reliably`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. High implied volatility clusters before and during turbulent periods. You are paid more because the claim is more likely and larger.`,`The direction is reversed — high implied volatility means options are expensive.`,`It raises the premium collected and typically raises the expected payout by at least as much.`,`It is observable from liquid option prices.`],explanation:`The nuance is that the premium is often high enough to be worth taking — the variance risk premium tends to widen in stress. The error is treating the high premium as free rather than as compensation, and sizing the position as though the risk had not also risen.`},{id:`der-vol-01-q5`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`volatility`,`portfolio`,`on-the-job`],concept:`vol-in-a-portfolio`,stem:`Which of these is NOT an implicit short volatility position?`,choices:[`Merger arbitrage`,`Trend following with long straddle-like characteristics`,`Selling credit protection`,`Fixed income relative value`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Merger arb collects a small spread and loses substantially on a deal break — short a deal-break option.`,`Correct. Trend following is typically long volatility: it loses small amounts in choppy markets and gains in sustained moves, which is the opposite payoff shape.`,`Collecting spread and taking the default is the classic short credit option.`,`Small convergence gains with a large loss on dislocation is the same shape.`],explanation:`This is why trend following is one of the few genuinely diversifying alternatives allocations: its payoff shape is opposite to most of the rest. It also explains its unpopularity — long volatility strategies lose money most of the time, which is what they are for.`},{id:`der-vol-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`volatility`,`stress`,`on-the-job`],concept:`vol-in-a-portfolio`,stem:`What single test best reveals hidden short volatility exposure across a multi-strategy portfolio?`,choices:[`A correlation matrix over the last five years`,`A common stress scenario — 20% equity drawdown, spreads doubling, liquidity gone — applied to every strategy`,`Comparing each strategy's Sharpe ratio`,`Reviewing each manager's stated strategy description`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Correlations computed in calm periods systematically miss exposures that only appear together in stress.`,`Correct. Every strategy that loses badly in the same scenario shares one exposure, whatever the labels say. It surfaces the aggregation that names and correlation matrices conceal.`,`High Sharpe ratios are a symptom of short volatility rather than a way of detecting it.`,`Descriptions use different vocabulary for identical payoff shapes, which is exactly the problem.`],explanation:`The output is a list of strategies that fail together, which is the real concentration in most alternatives portfolios. It is also actionable: it identifies which allocations are genuinely diversifying and which are the same bet with different paperwork.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:9,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
